WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.749
Avionics power starts on a 28 volt bus.

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:08.233
One that carries 80 volt surges, and 100 volt spikes.

00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:12.971
Yet every rail on the board must still come up in order.

00:00:14.021 --> 00:00:16.492
First, the surges stop at the door.

00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:24.115
The input stage clamps them, refuses a reversed supply, and shuts out any bus that drifts outside its range.

00:00:25.165 --> 00:00:33.055
Next, one buck brings the bus down, limiting current on every cycle, so a short downstream cannot run away.

00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:38.732
That one output then feeds four regulators.

00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:44.005
5 volts, 3.3, 1.8, and a last rail at 1.2 or 0.9.

00:00:44.005 --> 00:00:48.524
Each one ramps up gently, not all at once.

00:00:49.574 --> 00:00:53.153
Every rail reports back from inside a narrow window.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.732
Only then does the sequencer let the next one start.

00:00:57.782 --> 00:01:02.275
Inside the buck, current sense closes a fast inner loop.

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:07.891
Whether it holds steady from minus 55 to 125 degrees is not yet shown.

00:01:08.941 --> 00:01:17.600
And because one particle strike must never reorder a rail, the sequencer's logic is hardened, and triple-voted.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.861
None of this is silicon yet.

00:01:20.861 --> 00:01:22.836
Every figure is a target.

00:01:22.836 --> 00:01:27.022
The surge clamp, the loop margins, the upset spacing.

00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:29.470
All of it must still be proven.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:31.761
So bring us your bus profile.

00:01:31.761 --> 00:01:33.419
Bring your rail list.

00:01:33.419 --> 00:01:37.447
And we will tell you exactly what SKU-3 must prove.
